</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/20:_Carboxylic_Acids_and_Nitriles/20.05:_Preparing_Carboxylic_Acids" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Carboxylation_of_Grignard_Reagents"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-36554 editable">Carboxylation of Grignard Reagents</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-36554">In this method, an electrophilic halide is first transformed into a strongly nucleophilic Grignard reagent. The Grignard reagent adds to the C=O bond of carbon dioxide (an electrophile) to yield the salt of of a carboxylic acid called a halomagnesium carboxylate. This intermediate is then treated with a strong aqueous acid to form the carboxylic acid. Most alkyl or aryl halides can be used for this synthesis of carboxylic acids. The main limitation is that no functional groups incompatible with Grignard reagents can be present such has O-H, N-H, S-H, or C=O bonds.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36554"><img alt="20.5 grignard formation.svg" style="width: 304px; height: 94px;" width="304px" height="94px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/363190/20.5_grignard_formation.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=304&amp;height=94" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36554"><img alt="20.5 grignard formation steps.svg" style="width: 620px; height: 79px;" width="620px" height="79px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/363189/20.5_grignard_formation_steps.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=620&amp;height=79" /></p>

