</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/20:_Carboxylic_Acids_and_Nitriles/20.05:_Preparing_Carboxylic_Acids" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Hydrolysis_of_Nitriles"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-36554 editable">Hydrolysis of Nitriles</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-36554">Hydrolysis of nitriles to carboxylic acids requires two steps. First, an alkyl halide is reacted with a nucleophilic cyanide anion to form a nitrile intermediate by an S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction. Subsequent hydrolysis of the nitrile is typically performed by heating with an acidic or basic aqueous solution and uses a mechanism discussed later in this chapter. Due to the requirements of the S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction, only primary and secondary alkyl halides may be used for this reaction. Tertiary alkyl halides typically produce an alkene through E2 elimination reaction and aryl halides usually do not react.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36554"><img alt="20.5 nitrile hydrolysis.svg" style="width: 387px; height: 139px;" width="387px" height="139px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/363192/20.5_nitrile_hydrolysis.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=387&amp;height=139" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36554">Two steps of nitrile formation and hydrolysis</p>

